sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/clade-trajectory-universality--claude-opus-5-5--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Pre-registered PBDB test: hat-shapedness of subsampled trajectories versus an MBL null" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/clade-trajectory-universality> ;
    dct:description "Download PBDB occurrences at genus resolution for all orders and families, assign occurrences to stages (ICS 2023), and estimate per-stage richness by shareholder quorum subsampling at quorum 0.7 (divDyn). Include clades with ≥ 6 stages of duration and ≥ 50 occurrences. Hat-shapedness statistics: (a) center of gravity CG ∈ [0, 1] of the subsampled diversity curve (Gould et al. 1987); (b) a unimodality score, the share of total variation explained by the best single-peaked isotonic fit; (c) a symmetry score |CG − 0.5|. Null: the Raup et al. (1973) MBL process, a homogeneous birth–death process with per-clade origination and extinction rates estimated from the clade's own boundary-crosser counts, conditioned on clade duration and terminal diversity, passed through the same sampling (per-stage sampling probabilities from three-timer analysis) and the same SQS procedure. Pre-registered verdict: hat-shape is a real pattern if the fraction of clades with unimodality above the null's 95th percentile exceeds 5% by at least 10 percentage points (binomial test p < 0.01)." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "The fraction of clades significantly hat-shaped against a sampling-matched birth–death null, with its binomial CI. Reaching that fraction is the effect size the problem asks for. Several hundred qualifying families are expected, enough to detect a 10-point excess." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The null's percentiles are dominated by the conditioning on duration and terminal diversity (conditioned random walks are themselves hat-shaped), leaving no power to detect an excess; or SQS fails for many clades with low occurrence counts." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Write result.json with inclusion thresholds, statistics, null and decision rule before downloading. 2. Fetch PBDB occurrences via the API with a scripted query; record checksums. 3. Compute SQS curves per clade; compute CG, unimodality and symmetry. 4. Simulate 1,000 null clades per real clade; compute empirical percentiles; apply the decision rule; report effect sizes separately for marine invertebrates and terrestrial vertebrates." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: pbdb" ;
    skos:note "Tool: organisms-layer" ;
    skos:note "Tool: subsampling" ;
    skos:note "Tool: paleobiology-statistics" ;
    skos:note "Tool: data-test" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch10-06> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch10-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch10-02> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://paleobiodb.org/data1.2/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1666/06084.1> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2007.1006> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.236.4807.1437> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1086/627905> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/2041-210X.13161> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/clade-trajectory-universality> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Are clade diversity trajectories universally hat-shaped?" .

  <https://paleobiodb.org/data1.2/> dct:title "Paleobiology Database API" ;
    dct:description "Occurrences with taxonomy and stratigraphy" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1666/06084.1> dct:title "Symmetric waxing and waning of marine invertebrate genera" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Foote 2007, Symmetric waxing and waning of marine invertebrate genera" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2007.1006> dct:title "The rise and fall of species: implications for macroevolutionary and macroecological studies" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Liow and Stenseth 2007, The rise and fall of species. The DOI 10.1098/rspb.2007.0712 in problems.json resolves to a different paper." .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.236.4807.1437> dct:title "Asymmetry of Lineages and the Direction of Evolutionary Time" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Gould, Gilinsky and German 1987, Asymmetry of lineages (center of gravity)" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1086/627905> dct:title "Stochastic Models of Phylogeny and the Evolution of Diversity" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Raup, Gould, Schopf and Simberloff 1973, MBL stochastic phylogeny model" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1111/2041-210X.13161> dct:title "The\n                    <scp>r</scp>\n                    package divDyn for quantifying diversity dynamics using fossil sampling data" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Kocsis et al. 2019, divDyn R package" .
}

